{"id": "1.2-00019", "case_id": "github_evensgn_riscv_cpu_direct_v1", "parent_id": null, "source": "github/evensgn/risc-v-cpu", "record_protocol": null, "panels": {"stored": "<h2>Agent task and scoring</h2><div class='card'><table><tbody><tr><th style='width:175px'>Input task</th><td class='path'>Project specification, interfaces, and permitted public assets</td></tr><tr><th style='width:175px'>Agent context</th><td class='path'>Direct agent workspace: <a href='#task=1.2&amp;case=1.2-00019&amp;file_q=public%2F'>public/</a> (12 files)</td></tr><tr><th style='width:175px'>Expected output</th><td class='path'>Required RTL: src/cpu/cache.v, src/cpu/cpu.v, src/cpu/ctrl.v, src/cpu/mem_ctrl.v, src/cpu/reg_ex_mem.v, src/cpu/reg_id_ex.v, src/cpu/reg_if_id.v, src/cpu/reg_mem_wb.v and 12 more. <a href='#task=1.2&amp;case=1.2-00019&amp;file=public%2Foutput_contract.json'>Open full output contract</a></td></tr><tr><th style='width:175px'>Hidden reference</th><td class='path'>20 RTL files under private/reference/; <a href='#task=1.2&amp;case=1.2-00019&amp;file=private%2Freference%2Fsrc%2Fcpu%2Fcache.v'>open first reference file</a></td></tr><tr><th style='width:175px'>Private score</th><td class='path'>Passed frozen testpoints divided by total testpoints, using testbenches or formal verification<br><strong>1 frozen testpoints:</strong> test/sim_cpu.v. <a href='#task=1.2&amp;case=1.2-00019&amp;file=private%2Fscoring.json'>Open private scoring contract</a></td></tr></tbody></table></div><h2>Case-stored agent instruction</h2><div class='card'><p class='muted'>These are case-stored instructions. A launcher may add framing or execution limits; its saved trajectory is the exact runtime record.</p><details><summary>public/instruction.md · <a href='#task=1.2&amp;case=1.2-00019&amp;file=public%2Finstruction.md'>Open raw</a></summary><pre># Implement the Evensgn RISC-V CPU project\n\nRead the unchanged upstream README, doc/project-report.pdf and\ndoc/inst-supported.md under specification/original; project-report.txt is a\ntext extraction of the original report. The bundled RISC-V 2.2 manual provides\nISA definitions, while inst-supported.md fixes this project&#x27;s supported subset.\nImplement all 20 required RTL files (21 modules), headed by cpu. They include\nthe CPU pipeline, I/D caches, serial memory protocol, and UART queues.\n\nCurator configuration and interface clarifications from the pinned RTL:\n- This is the RV32I subset explicitly enumerated in inst-supported.md: ordinary\n  integer arithmetic, shifts, comparisons, branches/jumps, loads and stores.\n  It has 32 registers (x0 reads zero), 32-bit word data/address, and little-endian\n  byte memory. No M extension, compressed instructions, CSR/privileged execution,\n  exceptions or interrupts are required by this case. The five-stage pipeline\n  must preserve dependencies through forwarding and stalls; loads may require\n  a stall, and control-flow transfers discard wrong-path instruction work.\n- cpu has board ports EXCLK, active-low button, rst_led, serial Tx and Rx.\n  button assertion raises internal reset asynchronously; release takes two\n  internal clock edges. rst_led reflects reset. The clock primitive clk_wiz_0\n  is fixed environment support, not a generated module. It supplies the pinned\n  ideal 108MHz simulation clock corresponding to the source&#x27;s 100-to-108MHz\n  PLL template. The original report explicitly records FPGA bring-up problems;\n  this admission claims simulation qualification only, not FPGA correctness.\n- The reset PC output starts at zero and normal sequential instruction addresses\n  advance by four. The core has separate I/D cache request ports with rw flags\n  0=idle,1=read,2=write, 32-bit byte addresses/data, four byte enables, busy and\n  one-cycle done. Pipeline progress must wait for required memory completions.\n  Each cache instance selects INDEX_BIT=4,NASSOC=2 and default eight words/line.\n  Preserve read-miss refill, recent-use replacement, byte-masked write-through,\n  and disabled/explicit flush behavior; cache internals need not use source names.\n- CPU memory accesses travel over channel 0 of a two-channel multichan_trans.\n  mem_ctrl arbitrates its two request ports in increasing port priority, queues\n  pending requests while busy, and returns read data only on a received reply.\n  Read requests carry a 32-bit address in a five-byte message with read flag 0.\n  Write requests carry low32 data, next32 address, four byte-mask bits and write\n  flag 1 in a nine-byte message. Writes complete when the request is queued;\n  reads wait for the returned low32 data. The simulated host maps byte writes\n  to 0x104 to console output. Message integer fields are transmitted LSB first.\n- The multichannel packet format at PACKET_SIZE=8 is: start 100xxxxx with a\n  five-bit packet ID, channel 101xxxxx, byte length 110xxxxx, payload bytes\n  each containing a zero MSB plus the next seven low-to-high message bits,\n  then end 111xxxxx with matching ID. IDs increment modulo32. Per-channel\n  queues, readable/writable flags, and fair priority aging regulate flow;\n  accept complete matching packets and preserve their message lengths/data.\n- uart_trans sends an idle-high line, low start bit, eight LSB-first data bits,\n  even parity (XOR of data bits), then a high stop bit. Its declared default\n  CLOCKRATE=40000000 and BAUDRATE=2304000 yield integer bit interval17 clocks;\n  the bench preserves this ratio even though its actual internal clock is108MHz.\n  Do not substitute 8N1 or reinterpret the nominal parameter as physical baud.\n  RX samples bit centers, rejects bad parity/full receive queues, and presents\n  buffered data through recv_flag/receivable. TX uses send_flag/sendable queues.\n- reg_pc and the four pipeline-register stages propagate values or stall/bubble\n  them under ctrl. stage_id decodes and forwards operands; stage_ex computes\n  integer/branch results; stage_mem handles load extension/store byte masks.\n  regfile supplies register operands and writeback. riscv_cpu integrates these\n  with both caches. simple_ram is candidate-owned cache storage; it is included\n  once by cache.v, not supplied as a golden implementation. fifo, simo_fifo and\n  miso_fifo implement the queues used by UART and channel transport.\n\nFixed public support headers defines.v and utility.v contain ISA/constants and\nwidth arithmetic, with no RTL module implementation. The private bench uses the\nsame candidate UART/queue module types at its host endpoint; the original shared\ntopology is retained. Two optional cache diagnostic displays are omitted from\nthe synthesizable reference and preserved in original_reference. No functional\nassignment is changed. Use synthesizable RTL, no simulator output/file calls.\n\nScoring uses the entire original sim_cpu bench and shipped program as one point,\nrequiring its complete console output and normal finite termination. The old\ntest_bench wrapper has disconnected memory and is preserved as unavailable.\nThis finite program is not an exhaustive ISA/cache validation suite.\n</pre></details></div>"}, "files": [{"name": "case.json", "url": "assets/a2e6dc4f0d784f22c8a3b7d468d91c268094d443f142e059c6ab9d74e2431d9a.txt", "size": 23892, "binary": false, "section": "Other: case.json"}, {"name": "environment/runtime.json", "url": "assets/042a31c6308bb2375ee0639763fa1766f6cc3d3bfbdb698409cfda6fd5b7e1a5.txt", "size": 452, "binary": false, "section": "Other: environment"}, {"name": "private/direct_adapter.json", "url": "assets/0006163429a868a7ab854524db503a14ec18efa652578bb490d3d39fd0451fed.txt", "size": 1202, "binary": false, "section": "Private reference and oracle"}, {"name": "private/original_reference/src/cpu/cache.v", "url": "assets/2a65f9f4880fbc81db961b7cd890a4a738ed7d1277525e55d1466d5636bab3a1.txt", "size": 11457, "binary": false, "section": "Private reference and oracle"}, {"name": "private/original_reference/src/cpu/cpu.v", "url": "assets/1349b8e190